ek\" ($40). The card's \"Calculated amount\" = $58.33 corresponds to this biweek (11-24 Nov). The \"For next biweek\" = the next two-week segment (25 Nov - 4 Dec = 10 days) with remaining budget $40 → $40. That's plausible: the current biweek amount is fixed at the period start ($58.33), and the next biweek gets the remaining $40. Hmm, but $58.33 - spent? The current biweek had $60 spent (all on 13 Nov) > $58.33, so overspent; next biweek gets $40 (the entire remaining? no, remaining $40). Hmm.\n\nWait, \"Left of the total budget $100 - $60 = $40\" for the next biweek. That's remaining after all expenses. Then \"$40 / 10 days × 10 days = $40\" — the next biweek is 10 days (25 Nov - 4 Dec), so $40. OK. So the breakdown makes sense in its model: current biweek allocation was 14/24 of $100 = $58.33; next biweek gets the rest.\n\nAnd the \"Calculated amount\" is the current biweek's amount. So for Weekly, $29.17 for this week (11-17 Nov); next week would be remaining over 17 days. Let me verify the weekly breakdown: \"For next week $40 / 17 days × 7 days = $16.47\"? Let me check. This would confirm consistency.\n\nLet me select Weekly and open the breakdown."}
